syntax: parse the subscripts of associative arrays as string keys

Bash, zsh and ksh93 use the subscript of an associative array as a string
key, and only evaluate a subscript as arithmetic when the array is indexed,
which the shell decides at run time. The parser always parsed subscripts as
arithmetic expressions, so keys that are not valid arithmetic, which
bash -n and zsh -n accept, failed to parse:

	declare -A tree
	tree=([e/]=0 [d/e/]=1)
	echo "${tree[a.b]}"
	declare -A m=([^\(.*/\)?src/\(.*\)]=x [foo bar]=y)

The parser now records the names that a declaration declares after an
option containing A, like `declare -A`, `local -A`, `typeset -A` or
`declare -gA`, before parsing their values. The subscripts of those names,
in `${name[key]}`, `name[key]=value`, `name=([key]=value)` and arithmetic,
are parsed as words that end at the matching ']', keeping spaces. zsh
subscript flags such as `${name[(r)pattern]}` are still parsed as before.

Reproducer, before this change: `declare -A m=([e/]=0)` with LangBash failed
with "`/` must be followed by an expression".

